In order to define the divisor class group we have to associate to every $f \in K^{*}$ a “principal divisor” $	ext{div}(f)$ . We use the case of a Dedekind domain to guide us. There the principal ideal (f) was given by $$ (f) = \prod_ {\mathfrak {p}} \mathfrak {p} ^ {v _ {\mathfrak {p}} (f)}, $$ where $v_{p}: K^{*} 	o Z$ is the p-adic exponential valuation associated to the valuation ring $O_{p}$ . In general, $O_{p}$ is not anymore a discrete valuation ring. Nevertheless, $O_{p}$ defines a homomorphism $$ \operatorname{ord} _ {\mathfrak {p}}: K ^ {*} 	o \mathbb {Z} $$ which generalizes the valuation function. If $f = a / b \in K^*$ , with $a, b \in \mathcal{O}$ , then we put $$ \operatorname{ord} _ {\mathfrak {p}} (f) = \ell_ {\mathcal {O} _ {\mathfrak {p}}} (\mathcal {O} _ {\mathfrak {p}} / a \mathcal {O} _ {\mathfrak {p}}) - \ell_ {\mathcal {O} _ {\mathfrak {p}}} (\mathcal {O} _ {\mathfrak {p}} / b \mathcal {O} _ {\mathfrak {p}}), $$ where $\ell_{\mathcal{O}_{\mathfrak{p}}}(M)$ denotes the length of an $\mathcal{O}_{\mathfrak{p}}$ -module $M$ , i.e., the maximal length of a strictly decreasing chain $$ M = M _ {0} \supsetneqq M _ {1} \supsetneqq \dots \supsetneqq M _ {\ell} = 0 $$ of $\mathcal{O}_{\mathfrak{p}}$ -submodules. In the special case where $\mathcal{O}_{\mathfrak{p}}$ is a discrete valuation ring with maximal ideal $\mathfrak{m}$ , the value $
u = v_{\mathfrak{p}}(a)$ of $a \in \mathcal{O}_{\mathfrak{p}}$ , for $a 
eq 0$ , is given by the equation $$ a \mathcal {O} _ {\mathfrak {p}} = \mathfrak {m} ^ {
u}. $$ It is equal to the length of the $O_{p}$ -module $O_{p}/m^{
u}$ , because the longest chain of submodules is $$ \mathcal {O} _ {\mathfrak {p}} / \mathfrak {m} ^ {
u} \supset \mathfrak {m} / \mathfrak {m} ^ {
u} \supset \dots \supset \mathfrak {m} ^ {
u} / \mathfrak {m} ^ {
u} = (0). $$ Thus the function $ord_{p}$ agrees with the exponential valuation $v_{p}$ in this case. The property of the function $ord_{p}$ to be a homomorphism follows from the fact (which is easily proved) that the length function $\ell_{O_{p}}$ is multiplicative on short exact sequences of $O_{p}$ -modules. Using the functions $ord_{p}: K^{*} 	o Z$ , we can now associate to every element $f \in K^{*}$ the divisor $$ \operatorname{div} (f) = \sum_ {\mathfrak {p}} \operatorname{ord} _ {\mathfrak {p}} (f) \mathfrak {p}, $$ and thus obtain a canonical homomorphism $$ \operatorname{div}: K ^ {*} \longrightarrow D i v (\mathcal {O}). $$ The elements $	ext{div}(f)$ are called principal divisors. They form a subgroup $\mathcal{P}(\mathcal{O})$ of $	ext{Div}(\mathcal{O})$ . Two divisors D and $D'$ which differ only by a principal divisor are called rationally equivalent.

