This point of view suggests the further question: whether not only the “value” of the integer $f \in Z$ at p, but also the higher derivatives of f can be reasonably defined. In the case of the polynomials $f(z) \in \mathbb{C}[z]$ , the higher derivatives at the point z = a are given by the coefficients of the expansion $$ f (z) = a _ {0} + a _ {1} (z - a) + \dots + a _ {n} (z - a) ^ {n}, $$ and more generally, for rational functions $f(z) =  rac{g(z)}{h(z)} \in \mathbb{C}(z)$ , with $g, h \in \mathbb{C}[z]$ , they are defined by the Taylor expansion $$ f (z) = \sum_ {
u = 0} ^ {\infty} a _ {
u} (z - a) ^ {
u}, $$ provided there is no pole at z = a, i.e., as long as $(z - a) 
mid h(z)$ . The fact that such an expansion can also be written down, relative to a prime number p in Z, for any rational number $f \in Q$ as long as it lies in the local ring $$ \mathbb {Z} _ {(p)} = \left\{ rac {g}{h} \mid g, h \in \mathbb {Z}, p 
mid h ight\}, $$ leads us to the notion of p-adic number. First, every positive integer $f \in N$ admits a p-adic expansion $$ f = a _ {0} + a _ {1} p + \dots + a _ {n} p ^ {n}, $$ with coefficients $a_{i}$ in $\{0,1,\ldots,p-1\}$ , i.e., in a fixed system of representatives of the “field of values” $\kappa(p)=\mathbb{F}_{p}$ . This representation is clearly unique. It is computed explicitly by successively dividing by p, forming the following system of equations:

